WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.660
The same code built a motor neuron in your neck, and one in your lower back.

2
00:00:08.420 --> 00:00:09.420
What tells them apart?

3
00:00:10.433 --> 00:00:13.393
Position along the cord, written in Hox proteins.

4
00:00:14.467 --> 00:00:16.507
Motor neurons sort into columns.

5
00:00:17.387 --> 00:00:19.387
The medial column drives back muscles.

6
00:00:20.267 --> 00:00:23.207
The lateral column, at limb levels, drives limb muscles.

7
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:27.780
The preganglionic column projects to autonomic ganglia.

8
00:00:28.783 --> 00:00:33.303
Hox proteins act inside each cell, as repressors and activators.

9
00:00:34.283 --> 00:00:39.383
Hoxc6, at forelimb levels, and Hoxc9, at thoracic levels, repress each other.

10
00:00:40.367 --> 00:00:46.867
Predict. In chick, put Hoxc9 into forelimb-level progenitors, and so into the motor

11
00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:47.867
neurons they make.

12
00:00:48.587 --> 00:00:49.687
What column do they become?

13
00:00:55.650 --> 00:00:58.070
Their lateral column identity is suppressed.

14
00:00:59.010 --> 00:01:02.510
They take on a preganglionic identity, from a thoracic column.

15
00:01:03.483 --> 00:01:09.423
Timing matters. Hoxc9 given only after the last division suppresses the lateral

16
00:01:09.423 --> 00:01:12.363
column, but does not make preganglionic cells.

17
00:01:15.367 --> 00:01:18.967
Hox proteins work with an accessory factor, FoxP1.

18
00:01:19.747 --> 00:01:21.747
It gates the limb and autonomic columns.

19
00:01:22.767 --> 00:01:25.987
Predict. Delete Foxp1 in a mouse.

20
00:01:26.887 --> 00:01:28.747
Are limb-level motor neurons still made?

21
00:01:34.750 --> 00:01:37.830
Yes. Motor neurons are still made.

22
00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:41.090
What fails is the assignment of which one each is.

23
00:01:42.033 --> 00:01:44.613
They resemble the thoracic hypaxial column.

24
00:01:45.493 --> 00:01:49.213
In the limb, their axons appear to choose paths and muscles at random.

25
00:01:52.217 --> 00:01:54.717
Inside the limb column comes one more choice.

26
00:01:55.717 --> 00:01:58.597
Lateral cells make Lim1, and their axons go dorsal.

27
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:03.360
Medial cells keep Isl1, and their axons go ventral.

28
00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:07.660
Without Lim1, lateral axons lose their dorsal preference.

29
00:02:08.733 --> 00:02:13.453
Eph receptors and ephrins help carry out this choice, alongside other guidance

30
00:02:13.453 --> 00:02:14.453
systems.

31
00:02:14.983 --> 00:02:17.763
Run these layers forward, and you have a recipe.

32
00:02:18.863 --> 00:02:21.883
Retinoic acid gives mouse stem cells a spinal character.

33
00:02:22.883 --> 00:02:26.383
A Hedgehog agonist pushes them toward the motor neuron domain.

34
00:02:27.323 --> 00:02:28.523
Then motor neurons appear.

35
00:02:29.567 --> 00:02:31.687
Put into an embryo, they reach muscle.

36
00:02:32.487 --> 00:02:35.427
But this recipe gives mostly medial column cells.

37
00:02:36.247 --> 00:02:38.107
One named pool is still hard to make.

38
00:02:39.050 --> 00:02:44.130
Takeaway: overlapping layers set a motor neuron's class, its column, and its limb

39
00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:47.230
choice. Run them in order, and you have a recipe.
